```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Pendulum Simulator</title>
    <style>
      canvas {
        border: 1px solid black;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Pendulum Simulator</h1>
    <input id="initial-angle" type="number" value="45" placeholder="Initial Angle (degrees)">
    <button id="release-button">Release</button>
    <canvas id="canvas" width="400" height="400"></canvas>
    <script>
      const canvas = document.getElementById('canvas');
      const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
      const initialAngle = document.getElementById('initial-angle');
      const releaseButton = document.getElementById('release-button');
      const gravity = 9.81; // m/s^2
      let angle = 0; // degrees
      let velocity = 0; // m/s
      let period = 0; // s
      letisque = 0.05; // m

      function init() {
        angle = Math.PI * initialAngle.value / 180; // convert degrees to radians
        velocity = 0;
        period = 2 * Math.sqrt(1 / (gravity / (isque * Math.sin(angle) ** 2))); // period formula
        que = new Date().getTime();
      }

      function update() {
        const now = new Date().getTime();
        const deltaT = (now - que) / 1000; // convert to seconds
        que = now;
        velocity += -angle * 9.81 / period ** 2; // update velocity
        angle += velocity * deltaT; // update angle

        if (Math.abs(velocity) < 0.01) {
          angle = 0;
          velocity = 0;
        }

        // draw the pendulum
        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
        ctx.strokeStyle = 'black';
        ctx.lineWidth = 2;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(canvas.width / 2, canvas.height / 2, isque, canvas.height / 2 - isque, canvas.height / 2 - isque + Math.sin(angle) * isque);
        ctx.stroke();

        requestAnimationFrame(update);
      }

      releaseButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
        init();
        update();
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

This code creates a canvas element and uses the 2D context to draw a pendulum. The pendulum is represented by a circle connected to the top of the canvas by a line. The pendulum's angle is updated using the equations of motion for a simple pendulum, and the `requestAnimationFrame` function is used to call the `update` function repeatedly to simulate the pendulum's motion.

The user can input the initial angle of the pendulum in the input field and click the "Release" button to start the simulation. The pendulum will swing until it comes to rest.

