**Using the `XMLHttpRequest` object (older browsers)**

If you're targeting older browsers that don't support the Fetch API or XHR2, you can use the `XMLHttpRequest` object to make an HTTP request:
```javascript
var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('GET', 'https://example.com', true);
xhr.onload = function() {
  if (xhr.status === 200) {
    console.log(xhr.responseText);
  }
};
xhr.send();
```
**Using the Fetch API (modern browsers)**

The Fetch API is a newer way to make HTTP requests in JavaScript. It's supported in most modern browsers, including Chrome, Firefox, Edge, and Safari:
```javascript
fetch('https://example.com')
  .then(response => response.text())
  .then(data => console.log(data));
```
